WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.139 --> 00:00:07.780
In this part of our presentation, I'd like to talk about Unreal Motion Graphics, or UMG for short.

2
00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:18.420
Now, UMG is a tool that's used to create both the visuals and the functional part of any type of UI you wish to display.

3
00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:25.219
Now, UI can include anything from menus, whether those are full screen menus or pop-up menus, to HUDs or heads-up display.

4
00:00:25.219 --> 00:00:30.260
These are going to be things that convey information such as maybe the speed of a car or the health of a user,

5
00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:37.380
or even 3D info. So this might be if you walk up to maybe a computer and you press a button.

6
00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:41.140
You can even use UMG to create the buttons that you interact with.

7
00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:45.619
Now, this tool, UMG, is actually a combination of two different tools.

8
00:00:45.619 --> 00:00:50.659
There is the Designer, which I like to affectionately refer to as the tool where we make things pretty.

9
00:00:50.659 --> 00:00:56.659
This is where we do things like add buttons and set up where those buttons will be positioned on the screen.

10
00:00:57.299 --> 00:01:01.060
Then, inside of our event graph, this is where we make things functional.

11
00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:06.579
So we have all these beautiful buttons. This is where we actually program what those buttons do when they're clicked on.

12
00:01:06.579 --> 00:01:09.299
So let's pop over to the editor really quick and have a look at this.

13
00:01:09.299 --> 00:01:13.219
Now, here's our standard UMG widget, and again, here I have the Designer and the Graph.

14
00:01:13.219 --> 00:01:16.579
So let's take a look at the Designer's areas a little more closely.

15
00:01:16.579 --> 00:01:23.379
So over here in the palette, this is where we're going to find things that we want, such as buttons, scroll bars,

16
00:01:24.099 --> 00:01:26.900
slide bars, loading bars, all of that stuff.

17
00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:28.500
So what I'm going to do now is I'm going to take this button.

18
00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:30.420
I'm just going to drop it right here in the middle.

19
00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:31.859
And this is our view.

20
00:01:31.859 --> 00:01:35.780
So this is where we're going to make things look pretty.

21
00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:37.939
And this is also where we can do stuff like this.

22
00:01:37.939 --> 00:01:38.579
So here we go.

23
00:01:38.579 --> 00:01:40.420
I've put this button over here.

24
00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:43.379
Now, let's see that we wanted to add some text to this button.

25
00:01:43.379 --> 00:01:44.659
Now, there's a couple of things that I could do.

26
00:01:44.659 --> 00:01:49.459
I could take this text and I could also, you know, come here and try to place it on the button.

27
00:01:49.459 --> 00:01:51.540
But this leads to a couple of problems.

28
00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:54.659
So you can see here, that's not really ideal when I move that, it moves away.

29
00:01:54.659 --> 00:01:59.379
So another cool thing is over here in the hierarchy, what we can do is we can take this text

30
00:01:59.379 --> 00:02:02.019
and actually drop it on top of that button.

31
00:02:02.019 --> 00:02:05.700
And now whenever I move this button, that text is going to come along for the ride.

32
00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:09.469
Now, over here is the Details panel,

33
00:02:09.469 --> 00:02:13.550
and this is going to be context sensitive based off of what I have selected.

34
00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:16.430
So in this example, look, if I select the button here,

35
00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:19.710
I have everything for the button, like its color and its style,

36
00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:21.949
but I don't have access to the text.

37
00:02:21.949 --> 00:02:24.509
What I have to do is come here and click on the text.

38
00:02:24.509 --> 00:02:27.789
And now I can change this to maybe something like Start.

39
00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:29.219
So there we go.

40
00:02:29.219 --> 00:02:30.659
I have my Start button.

41
00:02:30.659 --> 00:02:35.139
Now I can also do cool stuff like grab this button 25 and duplicate it.

42
00:02:35.139 --> 00:02:38.819
So I could have this Start and then maybe I have this other one over here.

43
00:02:38.819 --> 00:02:40.659
Maybe we call this one Quit.

44
00:02:40.659 --> 00:02:42.659
So here we go, Q-U-I-T.

45
00:02:43.219 --> 00:02:45.219
And there we go, we have our Start and our Quit.

46
00:02:45.219 --> 00:02:48.740
Now, the other cool thing about the UMG tool,

47
00:02:48.819 --> 00:02:51.780
again, is the event graph.

48
00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:56.099
Now I'm going to grab this Quit button here and I'm just going to come over here

49
00:02:56.099 --> 00:02:58.340
and we're going to scroll all the way down to this On Clicked

50
00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:00.419
and we're going to hit this plus sign right there.

51
00:03:00.419 --> 00:03:04.340
And notice that it's actually taking me over to the event graph.

52
00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:07.539
And again, here's my designer and here is my graph.

53
00:03:07.539 --> 00:03:10.340
Now the graph is what makes this functional.

54
00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:15.939
So if I was just to come here and just do print, do a print string, we'll say hello.

55
00:03:15.939 --> 00:03:18.099
Now I'm just going to compile and save this.

56
00:03:18.099 --> 00:03:21.139
And I have it preset up so that when I go ahead and press play here,

57
00:03:21.139 --> 00:03:23.060
you can see there's my start and my quit.

58
00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:25.060
So if I hit quit, you can see it says hello.

59
00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:30.289
And that's how easy things are to set up using UMG.

60
00:03:30.289 --> 00:03:31.889
That's why this tool is so powerful,

61
00:03:31.889 --> 00:03:35.409
because we can quickly set things up inside of our designer here,

62
00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:42.020
give our buttons different functionalities by coming over here and with the events.

63
00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:43.860
You can see here we have it On Clicked and On Pressed.

64
00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:47.379
But even if I was to come here for maybe grab an analog slider here,

65
00:03:47.379 --> 00:03:53.919
we can do something like grab this and say on analog begin mouse capture.

66
00:03:53.919 --> 00:03:54.400
Let's do that.

67
00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:58.240
We'll print to this string again and let's just compile and save.

68
00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:00.639
And you'll see how simple it is to do stuff with this,

69
00:04:00.639 --> 00:04:03.599
which is why this is such a powerful tool.

70
00:04:03.599 --> 00:04:05.120
You can see it's saying hello right there.

71
00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:09.099
So again, it's working just the way that we want it.

72
00:04:09.099 --> 00:04:13.259
We also have the full access to the standard blueprint debugger

73
00:04:13.259 --> 00:04:15.259
so we can see what's going on inside of here.

74
00:04:15.259 --> 00:04:17.100
You can see the execution happening there.

75
00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:22.220
So again, this is Unreal Motion Graphics and this tool is what we utilize

76
00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:26.220
to help make our UI's both beautiful as well as interactive.
